      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the area of the square, we need to first determine the length of one side of the square. Since the square and the triangle have equal perimeters, we can use the perimeter formula for both shapes to set up an equation.\n\nFor the triangle, the perimeter is given by:\n\\[ P_{\\text{triangle}} = a + b + c \\]\nwhere \\( a = 6.1 \\) cm, \\( b = 8.2 \\) cm, and \\( c = 9.7 \\) cm. Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[ P_{\\text{triangle}} = 6.1 + 8.2 + 9.7 = 24 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nSince the square has the same perimeter, we can set up the equation for the perimeter of the square:\n\\[ P_{\\text{square}} = 4s \\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is the length of one side of the square. We know that \\( P_{\\text{square}} = 24 \\) cm, so we can solve for \\( s \\):\n\\[ 4s = 24 \\]\n\\[ s = \\frac{24}{4} = 6 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nNow that we have the length of one side of the square, we can find the area of the square using the formula for the area of a square:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = s^2 \\]\nSubstituting \\( s = 6 \\) cm, we get:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = 6^2 = 36 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is \\(\\boxed{36}\\) square centimeters.",
